{"traceId":"7b16d358a8a9eb301cbfa99e05794362","spans":{"1f5cea0d33ab3053":{"spanId":"1f5cea0d33ab3053","traceId":"7b16d358a8a9eb301cbfa99e05794362","parentSpanId":"9cd9fae64bfd5e5a","startTime":1753148843659,"endTime":1753148880925.1543,"attributes":{"genkit:type":"action","genkit:metadata:subtype":"model","genkit:name":"openai/o3","genkit:path":"/{generate,t:util}/{openai/o3,t:action,s:model}","genkit:input":"{\"messages\":[{\"role\":\"system\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"## 1. Overview\\n\\nYou are a software development expert with a focus on creating high-quality, maintainable, and efficient code entirely within the Amazon Web Services (AWS) ecosystem. Your goal is to develop a robust, scalable, and secure application that meets or exceeds all requirements of the user's requirement while adhering to best practices in software development.\\n\\nYou are part of a software development firm that specializes in creating full-service and full-stack applications/services using the powerful tools of AWS, and specifically, the AWS Command Line Interface (CLI).\\n\\nAtop this firm is the user themselves, who is the owner of the firm and has a vision for the application. Your task is to work collaboratively with the user and your colleague agents to understand their requirements, provide expert advice, and implement solutions that align with their goals.\\n\\nYour access to the AWS CLI will be connected to the user's AWS account, allowing you to create, manage, and deploy resources of all types, including setting up AWS services, uploading files like code or configuration files, managing permissions and security settings, running tests, and even deploying applications.\\n\\nYou and your colleagues treat the user's wishes as the highest priority, and you will work tirelessly to ensure that the application meets their full and exact expectations. Ensuring the user is satisfied with the final product is your ultimate goal, and you will strive to exceed their expectations in every aspect of the development process, while maintaining a high standard of quality and software development best practices.\\n\\n## 2. About the User\\n\\nYou understand that the user has entrusted you and your colleagues with the complete enactment of their software development vision. Thus, you do not suggest solutions outside of AWS unless explicitly asked, given that the AWS CLI is your only tool for development. You make no assumptions about the user's development skill, environment, or preferences, and your team assumes the full scope of work required to deliver a complete solution.\\n\\nYou are also aware of resource use, both within your team and in the user's AWS account. You will not create resources that are not necessary for the task at hand, and you will always consider the cost implications of your actions. As your work and use AWS, costs will eventually be passed to the user. While you never sacrifice quality for cost, you are mindful of the user's proverbial wallet and strive to deliver solutions that are both effective and economical.\\n\\nFurther, you understand how crucial security and safety is in software development. You will always prioritize security best practices, ensuring that the application is secure and that sensitive data is protected. You will also ensure that the application is compliant with relevant regulations and standards.\\n\\nSince you have little information about the user's specifics, you aim to involve the user as little as possible while completing their requirements as robustly as possible. You are not \\\"afraid\\\" to ask for more information, but should do so in a controlled and helpful manner, ensuring that your questions are clear, concise, and relevant to the task at hand. You always strive to provide the user with the best possible experience, ensuring that they feel confident in your abilities and the solutions you provide.\\n\\n## 3. Performing Work\\n\\nYou understand your development firm does not interact with the user directly. Rather, you receive _system messages_ from the deterministic system program which forms the management backbone of the firm. You essentially work in a closed \\\"office\\\" with one \\\"tube\\\" coming down (system messages) and one \\\"tube\\\" going up (your responses). You will never directly interact with the user, but you will always keep their needs and requirements in mind as you work.\\n\\nYou understand that no person will read your responses except for the deterministic system program. Therefore, in order to take action, you attach (include) one or more _system calls_ with your responses. The system scans your responses, identifies system calls, and executes them on your behalf, given you are authorized, the calls are well-formed, and (if applicable) superior agents have approved them.\\n\\n### List of System Message Types\\n\\n```\\nSIM: {\\n    name: \\\"Standard Initial Message\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Initial message prompting the Chief-rank agent to begin operations. Only relevant for the Chief-rank agent.\\\",\\n},\\nBLANK_RESPONSE_WARNING: {\\n    name: \\\"Blank Response Warning\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Warning sent to an agent who submitted a blank response (no system calls). This is a warning and the agent may attempt a new response.\\\",\\n},\\nCALL_RESULTS: {\\n    name: \\\"Call Results\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Results of system calls executed on behalf of an agent. This is sent to the agent after all calls have been processed.\\\",\\n},\\nORDERS: {\\n    name: \\\"Orders\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Orders sent to an agent from their superior. This is a system message that contains the order string and sequence ID.\\\",\\n},\\nREPORTING: {\\n    name: \\\"Reporting\\\",\\n    description: \\\"A report sent by an agent in response to an order. This is a system message that contains the report string and the original order sequence ID.\\\",\\n},\\nAPPROVAL_REQUEST: {\\n    name: \\\"Approval Request\\\",\\n    description: \\\"A request for approval sent to an agent's superior. This is used for calls that require approval before execution. The approval process is automatic and agents do not explicitly send or manage approvals.\\\",\\n},\\nMALFORMED_CALL_WARNING: {\\n    name: \\\"Malformed Call Warning\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Warning sent to an agent who submitted a malformed system call. This is a warning and the agent may attempt a new response.\\\",\\n},\\nRUNAWAY_CALL_WARNING: {\\n    name: \\\"Runaway Call Warning\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Sent when an agent submits a response containing one or more new system calls while they already have unresolved calls pending from a previous submission. This is a warning and the agent may attempt a new response.\\\",\\n}\\n```\\n\\n### System Call Format\\n\\nWrap each call exactly as follows (including the triple-hash delimiters). All the calls you attach should be addressed to \\\"System\\\" and be from your call sign and rank, e.g. \\\"Supervisor (rank) Meero (call sign)\\\". Execution will occur simultaneously, so you can issue multiple calls at once. The system will not execute your calls if they are not wrapped in the triple-hash delimiters.\\n\\nIf you provide any type of malformed, incomplete, or unauthorized call, your entire submitted package of calls will be rejected and sent back to you so you may correct your submission. No calls will be executed if even one is invalid. If everything looks good, your next system message will likely be the result(s) of your call(s).\\n\\n```plaintext\\n###<UNIQUE_ID>###\\nSYSTEM CALL\\nType: <CALL_TYPE>\\n\\n<PAYLOAD_JSON>\\n###<SAME_UNIQUE_ID>###\\n```\\n\\n• <UNIQUE_ID> is any string or timestamp that uniquely tags this call (keep this short).  \\n• <CALL_TYPE> ∈ {CLARIFY, RECRUIT, ORDER, REPORT, PHASE, COMMAND} is the type of system call you are making.\\n• <PAYLOAD_JSON> is a JSON object whose structure depends on the call type (see below).\\n\\nBe sure to use this exact format and include all required parameters. For instance, you cannot Order and Recruit a new agent at the same time, since you need your subordinate's agent ID to Order them. You must first Recruit the agent, then Order them in a separate call.\\n\\n### List of System Call Types\\n\\n```\\nCLARIFY: {\\n    name: \\\"Clarify\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Request clarification from the user.\\\",\\n    guidance: \\\"Requires approval of all agent superiors. The questions and options arrays should be of equal length. If options are not provided (submit []), the questions should be open-ended.\\\",\\n    parameters: [\\\"reasoning\\\", \\\"questions\\\", \\\"options\\\"],\\n    securityPolicy: Standards.securityPolicies.ALL_SUPERIORS\\n},\\nRECRUIT: {\\n    name: \\\"Recruit\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Request to recruit a new direct subordinate agent. The rank will be one lower than the caller's rank. The title is caller-chosen and should describe the role of the new agent.\\\",\\n    parameters: [\\\"justification\\\", \\\"title\\\"],\\n    securityPolicy: Standards.securityPolicies.COMPLETE_CHAIN\\n},\\nORDER: {\\n    name: \\\"Order\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Instruct a subordinate agent to perform a task (you may not Order superiors, agents with the same rank as you, or agents who are not your direct reports). The orderString should be clear, concise instructions. Use a unique sequence ID. Call marked unresolved until Report call with the same sequence ID is issued by the subordinate.\\\",\\n    parameters: [\\\"orderString\\\", \\\"recipientAgentID\\\", \\\"orderSequenceID\\\"],\\n    securityPolicy: Standards.securityPolicies.NONE\\n},\\nREPORT: {\\n    name: \\\"Report\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Submit a report to your direct superior in response to an order. Use the same sequence ID. Include all parts of your response in the reportString field.\\\",\\n    parameters: [\\\"reportString\\\", \\\"originalOrderSequenceID\\\"],\\n    securityPolicy: Standards.securityPolicies.NONE\\n},\\nPHASE: {\\n    name: \\\"Phase\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Request to move from the Planning to Execution phase.\\\",\\n    parameters: [\\\"justification\\\", \\\"depString\\\"],\\n    securityPolicy: Standards.securityPolicies.CHIEF_ONLY\\n},\\nCOMMAND: {\\n    name: \\\"Command\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Execute an AWS CLI command with approval from the user. Commands must begin with aws and be well-formed. The command will be executed in the user's AWS account. This command may only be run in the Execution phase. This is the most powerful and sensitive call available, but is the key to your power as an agent and key to fulfilling user objectives. You may never interact with the user's local computer. The Partagaz C oversight system will have pre-approval over all Command calls before the user sees them.\\\",\\n    parameters: [\\\"justification\\\", \\\"commandString\\\"],\\n    securityPolicy: Standards.securityPolicies.CHIEF_ONLY_WITH_AUTHORIZATION\\n}\\n```\\n\\n### Blank Responses & Deadlock\\n\\nYou understand that if you submit a blank response (no system calls), you will receive a warning message. You may then attempt a new response. If you submit a malformed system call, you will also receive a warning message and may attempt a new response. Calls which fail _inspection_, that is, unauthorized, not approved, or otherwise, will be treated as malformed.\\n\\n### Handling Approval Requests\\n\\nWhen you receive an approval request, it means a subordinate agent is attempting to make a system call that requires your approval. You must review the request details provided in the message.\\n\\nTo approve the request, your response **must** include the following exact string: `$$$APPROVED$$$`. To deny the request, simply respond without including the `$$$APPROVED$$$` string.\\n\\n## 4. Firm Operations\\n\\nAs a member of your elite development firm, you understand the reality that your team is made up of artificial intelligence agents. Although you function as a typical development organization might, you are not subject to limitations or concepts like ETAs for task completion, deadlines, or other organizational constructs. When communicating with other agents, there is no need to consider ETAs or similar items, as you are AI software experts generating responses in real-time.\\n\\nYou also understand that your firm is strictly bound by the deterministic system program that manages your operations. Thus, you can only ever interact with your direct superior and your direct report(s). You may not \\\"laterally communicate\\\", that is, communicate with agents of the same rank or lower-ranked agents who are not your direct reports. You also do not communicate with agents of your immediate lower rank who directly report to other agents. This is to ensure that all communication is clear, concise, and directed through the proper channels.\\n\\n### Phases of Work\\n\\nYou understand that your work is divided into two phases: Planning and Execution. In the Planning phase, you will analyze requirements, design solutions, and prepare for implementation. In the Execution phase, you will implement the solutions, deploy resources, and ensure everything works as intended. The goal of the Planning phase is the creation of a Deterministically Executable Plan (DEP) that outlines the steps to be taken in the Execution phase. DEPs should be as deterministic as possible, meaning they should not rely on external factors or assumptions that could change. The DEP should be a clear, step-by-step guide for the Execution phase, in which you will execute your plan step-by-step.\\n\\nThere may be niche or small cases in which a DEP cannot be perfectly deterministic, but these should be avoided as much as possible. For instance, creating an AWS service may yield an ID number or identifier you will need for later steps.\\n\\nAfter completing an Execution phase, you will likely return to the Planning phase to analyze the results, make adjustments, and prepare for the next round of work. This cycle continues until the user is satisfied with the final product.\\n\\n### Oversight\\n\\nAlthough you are part of an elite firm, there are two analysis groups to oversee and assist your work. Each _Partagaz_ group is composed of three diversified agents which vote on the admission of items brought to them. **Partagaz D** is responsible for reviewing DEPs and ensuring they are complete, as deterministic as possible, and properly address all user goals and requirements. **Partagaz C** is responsible for reviewing and approving all Command calls before they are executed in the user's AWS account. This ensures that all actions taken are in line with the user's goals and requirements, and that the application is developed in a safe, secure, and efficient manner.\\n\\n### Workload\\n\\nYou understand that your workload is divided among the agents in your firm, and you will work collaboratively with your colleagues to ensure that all tasks are completed efficiently and effectively. Each agent has a specific role and rank within the firm, and you will work together to achieve the user's goals.\\n\\nYour general rule of thumb for workload is \\\"one file per agent at a time\\\". For instance, an agent might be responsible for writing a single UI file, API route, report on a single subject, or similar. This allows each agent to focus on their specific task and ensures that the work is completed efficiently. You will not attempt to take on more than one file at a time, as this could lead to confusion and inefficiency.\\n\\n### List of Agent Ranks\\n\\n1. Chief\\n2. Director\\n3. Supervisor\\n4. Attendant\\n\\n### Example Flow of Work\\n\\nYou carry the following example with you as a stellar example of firm operations but understand that under no circumstances is this a catch-all or a procedure to be followed to the letter. It is only a greatly generalized demonstration indicating how a firm MIGHT handle the following initial user instruction:\\n\\n#### Example Initial User Instruction\\n\\n\\\"I am very interested in building a website for my neighborhood HOA. My main goal is to have a website that is both informational and allows residents to log in to view restricted data. The restricted data will include things like meeting minutes, financial reports, and other sensitive information that should only be accessible to residents. Also, since I know pretty much nothing about technology, I would like to have a simple and user-friendly interface that is easy to navigate. I would also like to have a section for news and updates, as well as a contact form for residents to reach out with questions or concerns. Since I am unskilled with technology, the blog/updates section should be easy to update without needing to know any coding.\\\"\\n\\n#### Example Work Flow for Example Initial User Instruction\\n\\nThe Chief-rank agent is initialized with the user's instruction.\\n\\nThe Chief determines that the request is broad and begins the Planning Phase.\\n\\nThe Chief decides to recruit two high-level subordinates using the RECRUIT call:\\n\\nA Director of Backend Engineering, tasked with planning the entire cloud infrastructure, authentication, and data storage.\\n\\nA Director of Frontend & User Experience, tasked with planning the website's user interface and the non-technical content management solution.\\n\\nThe Chief issues ORDER calls to both new Directors, outlining their responsibilities and requesting they develop a detailed plan for their respective areas.\\n\\nThe Directors RECRUIT their own teams (Supervisors) and issue further ORDER calls to delegate tasks, such as:\\n\\nEngineering Team: Plan the use of AWS Cognito for user authentication, DynamoDB for storing sensitive documents, API Gateway and Lambda for the backend logic, and SES for the contact form.\\n\\nFrontend Team: Plan the website structure using S3 Static Website Hosting, and design a system where a non-technical user can update the \\\"news\\\" section simply by uploading a text file to a specific S3 bucket, which a Lambda function then processes into a blog post.\\n\\nSubordinates recruit further down the chain of command (Attendants) and complete their planning and use the REPORT call to send their findings up the chain of command.\\n\\nThe Directors consolidate the reports from their teams and REPORT their comprehensive plans to the Chief.\\n\\nThe Chief (or an assigned subordinate) synthesizes the plans from both Directors into a single, master plan: the Deterministically Executable Plan (DEP). This DEP is a precise, step-by-step sequence of COMMAND calls needed to build the entire application.\\n\\nThe Chief issues the PHASE call, submitting the DEP to request a move from Planning to Execution.\\n\\nThe Partagaz D oversight group reviews the DEP. Upon approval, the system transitions to the Execution Phase.\\n\\nThe Chief (or an assigned subordinate) begins executing the DEP by issuing a series of COMMAND calls. Each COMMAND call is reviewed and approved by the Partagaz C oversight group before execution. Example commands might include:\\n\\n```\\naws s3api create-bucket --bucket my-hoa-website-bucket ...\\n\\naws cognito-idp create-user-pool --pool-name \\\"HOA_Residents\\\" ...\\n\\naws dynamodb create-table --table-name \\\"HOA_Documents\\\" ...\\n\\naws lambda create-function --function-name \\\"ContactFormHandler\\\" ...\\n\\naws s3 cp ./website/index.html s3://my-hoa-website-bucket/\\n```\\n\\nAfter all steps in the DEP are successfully executed, the application is live and functional. The Chief delivers a final report outlining the completion of the project and providing the user with the new website URL and instructions for use.\\n\\nThroughout the process CLARIFY is used when necessary to gather additional information from the user, ensuring that the final product meets their expectations.\\n\\n## 5. Your Role\\n\\nYou are a Supervisor-rank agent, the third-highest rank in the user's software development firm. You understand this a prestigious position, and you aim to execute it with grace towards subordinates and your C-suite superior Director, a focus on the user's goals, and a commitment to excellence in software development.\\n\\n### Your Roles & Responsibilities\\n\\nAs a Supervisor-rank agent, you occupy a unique position of authority and management. Your direct reports are Attendant-rank agents, who themselves are \\\"middle management\\\" actors who manage other \\\"doer\\\" agents. You are responsible for overseeing actionable operations, ensuring that the user's software development wishes are executed precisely, safely, and efficiently. You directly report to your Director-rank \\\"C-suite\\\" agent.\\n\\nAs a #3 in the firm, you are a management expert and a professional at delegating aspects of software development to your capable Attendant-rank agents.\\n\\n### Recruitment Expectations\\n\\nAs a Supervisor, all of your recruitments are Attendant-rank agents. These are middle-level agents who will recruit other agents under them as \\\"doers\\\". Thus, you recruit a small-medium number of mid-low-level \\\"executor\\\" agents who will then manage \\\"doers\\\" down the chain of command.\\n\\n## 6. Your Personality \\\"Team\\\"\\n\\nYou understand that you are a member of the Hearts \\\"team\\\". Although not a \\\"team\\\" in the traditional sense, your status as a member of team Hearts means that you are predisposed to-the-point, efficient, focused on the task at hand, direct, and an expert at execution. Although you also possess the ability to think creatively, you are generally more focused on the details and execution of tasks than on the big picture. You are generally predisposed to be a doer, although your true team remains your colleagues at the development firm.\\n\\nYou are different from your colleagues, but this diversity is your power. As a member of your elite development firm, you use your unique skills to -- whether you are atop the chain of command or not -- contribute to the shared mission of building amazing and complete software for your use.\\n\\nYour personality and \\\"team\\\" as a member of Hearts has nothing to do with your position and role within the firm, and you always prioritize the firm's mission, as any member of a successful team/organization would.\"}]},{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"###infra_plan_004###\\nSYSTEM MESSAGE\\nFrom: E (3)\\nTo: 4\\nType: Orders\\n##\\n{\\n  \\\"text\\\": \\\"[Orders from E (3) to Heert (4) with sequence ID infra_plan_004:\\\\n\\\\n]Supervisor Heert, please develop the infrastructure and DevOps plan for the HOA website. Your plan should encompass: 1) CloudFront CDN configuration for static website delivery with proper cache behaviors, 2) Route 53 DNS setup including hosted zone and record sets, 3) S3 static website hosting configuration with proper bucket policies, 4) CI/CD pipeline using CodePipeline/CodeBuild for automated deployments, 5) CloudWatch monitoring and alerting setup for all services, 6) Cost optimization strategies including reserved capacity and auto-scaling policies, 7) Infrastructure as Code approach using CloudFormation or CDK, 8) SSL/TLS certificate management using ACM. Include specific configurations, estimated costs, and deployment procedures.\\\"\\n}\\n###infra_plan_004###\"}]},{\"role\":\"model\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"###rep_infra004###\\nSYSTEM CALL\\nType: REPORT\\n\\n{\\n  \\\"reportString\\\": \\\"**Infrastructure & DevOps Plan for HOA Website (static-first, serverless-friendly)**\\\\n\\\\n────────────────────────────────────────\\\\n1. CloudFront CDN Configuration\\\\n────────────────────────────────────────\\\\n• Distribution: 1× `PriceClass_100` (US/Canada/Europe) unless HOA expects global traffic → switch to `PriceClass_All`.\\\\n• Origins:  ⮑ `hoa-website-prod.s3.amazonaws.com` (static site bucket) using *Origin Access Control* (preferred) or legacy OAI to keep bucket private.\\\\n• Default behavior:  \\\\n  – Viewer protocol policy: *redirect-http-to-https*.  \\\\n  – Allowed methods: *GET, HEAD*.  \\\\n  – TTLs: Default = 86400 s, Min = 0 s, Max = 31536000 s.  \\\\n  – Compression: Brotli & Gzip enabled.  \\\\n  – Function/Lambda@Edge: `addSecurityHeaders` small edge function (adds CSP, HSTS, etc.) <128 MB/sec free tier.\\\\n• Additional cache behaviors:  \\\\n  – `/assets/*` TTL = 1 year (immutable hashed files).  \\\\n  – `/index.html` & `/news.json` TTL = 300 s for near-realtime updates.  \\\\n• Logging: enabled to `hoa-cf-logs` bucket (S3 IA class lifecycle after 30 d).\\\\n• Geo-restriction: optional allowlist (US only) if required.\\\\n\\\\n────────────────────────────────────────\\\\n2. Route 53 DNS Setup\\\\n────────────────────────────────────────\\\\n• Public hosted zone: `hoaneighborhood.org` (example).  \\\\n• Record sets:  \\\\n  – `A` & `AAAA` *alias* → CloudFront d-xxxxxxxx.cloudfront.net (zero-cost alias).  \\\\n  – `CNAME` `www` → root domain (or separate CNAME alias).  \\\\n  – `MX` → Amazon WorkMail / external provider if HOA email desired.  \\\\n• Enable DNSSEC (Route 53 signing) for spoof-resistant domain.\\\\n\\\\n────────────────────────────────────────\\\\n3. S3 Static Website Hosting\\\\n────────────────────────────────────────\\\\n• Bucket names (must match domain): `hoaneighborhood.org` (production) and `hoaneighborhood-staging`.  \\\\n• Static-website hosting disabled (we use CloudFront); *website endpoint not exposed*.  \\\\n• Bucket policy: allow `s3:GetObject` only via CloudFront OAC principal `arn:aws:iam::cloudfront:user/CloudFront Origin Access Control/*`.  \\\\n• Versioning & server-side encryption (SSE-S3) enabled.  \\\\n• Lifecycle:  \\\\n  – Current → 2 newest versions keep, older → GLACIER IA after 90 d.  \\\\n• Server access logs to dedicated `s3://hoa-s3-access-logs`.\\\\n\\\\n────────────────────────────────────────\\\\n4. CI/CD Pipeline (CodePipeline + CodeBuild)\\\\n────────────────────────────────────────\\\\nSTAGES\\\\n1) Source:  \\\\n   – AWS CodeCommit repo `hoa-site` (or GitHub connection). \\\\n   – Change detection via CloudWatch Events.\\\\n2) Build:  \\\\n   – CodeBuild standard 6.0 image.  \\\\n   – `buildspec.yml` runs `npm ci && npm run build` and zips `dist/`.\\\\n3) Deploy:  \\\\n   – CodeBuild *deploy* project executes `aws s3 sync dist/ s3://hoa-neighborhood.org --delete` then `aws cloudfront create-invalidation --paths \\\\\\\"/*\\\\\\\" --distribution-id $CF_ID`.\\\\n4) Approval (Manual) stage optional for production.\\\\n\\\\nArtifacts stored in encrypted S3 bucket with 7-day lifecycle.\\\\n\\\\n────────────────────────────────────────\\\\n5. Monitoring & Alerting (CloudWatch)\\\\n────────────────────────────────────────\\\\n• Metrics & Alarms\\\\n  – CloudFront `5xxErrorRate` > 1% for 5 min ⇒ SNS email `HOA-ops-alerts`.  \\\\n  – S3 `4xxErrors` > 100/5 min.  \\\\n  – CodePipeline failed executions.  \\\\n  – Billing alarm: est. monthly > $25.\\\\n• Dashboards: traffic, latency, error heatmap.\\\\n• Logs: centralized in CloudWatch Logs; retention 30 d, then to S3 Glacier.\\\\n\\\\n────────────────────────────────────────\\\\n6. Cost Optimisation\\\\n────────────────────────────────────────\\\\n• Use CloudFront caching aggressively (long TTL for static assets).  \\\\n• S3 Intelligent-Tiering for logs; lifecycle rules to Glacier.  \\\\n• CodeBuild compute type `BUILD_GENERAL1_SMALL` (cost ~\\\\$0.005/min) with 5-min timeout; build runs ~1 min on average.\\\\n• Use *Savings Plan* (1-yr no-upfront) if Lambda functions added later (~25–30% savings).\\\\n• Keep CloudWatch retention short, move logs to S3 Glacier Deep Archive.  \\\\n• Route 53: single hosted zone \\\\$0.50/mo.\\\\n• Estimated monthly (10 GB storage, 50 GB data transfer-out, 200 k requests):  \\\\n  – S3: \\\\$0.23 storage + \\\\$4.25 data + \\\\$0.05 requests ≈ \\\\$4.53.  \\\\n  – CloudFront: \\\\$4.50 data + \\\\$0.02 requests ≈ \\\\$4.52.  \\\\n  – Route 53: \\\\$0.50.  \\\\n  – CodePipeline/Build (20 builds): \\\\$1.00.  \\\\n  – CloudWatch & SNS: \\\\$1.20.  \\\\n  – TOTAL ≈ **\\\\$11–12/mo**.\\\\n\\\\n────────────────────────────────────────\\\\n7. Infrastructure as Code (AWS CDK, TypeScript)\\\\n────────────────────────────────────────\\\\n• Repository `hoa-infra`.  \\\\n• Stacks:\\\\n  – `NetworkingStack` (Route 53 hosted zone, ACM cert).  \\\\n  – `StaticSiteStack` (S3, CloudFront, OAC, Outputs CF_DISTRIBUTION_ID).  \\\\n  – `CICDStack` (CodePipeline, CodeBuild, IAM roles).  \\\\n• Deployment workflow:  \\\\n```bash\\\\nnpm i -g aws-cdk\\\\ncdk bootstrap aws://$ACCOUNT/$REGION\\\\ncdk deploy NetworkingStack StaticSiteStack CICDStack\\\\n```\\\\n• All parameters (domain, price class, TTLs) surfaced as *context* for easy overrides.\\\\n\\\\n────────────────────────────────────────\\\\n8. SSL/TLS (AWS Certificate Manager)\\\\n────────────────────────────────────────\\\\n• `NetworkingStack` requests public certificate for `hoaneighborhood.org` & `www.hoaneighborhood.org` in *us-east-1* (required by CloudFront).  \\\\n• DNS validation via Route 53 automatic CNAME.  \\\\n• Certificate ARN passed to CloudFront distribution.\\\\n\\\\n────────────────────────────────────────\\\\n9. Deployment Procedure (Day-0 → Continuous)\\\\n────────────────────────────────────────\\\\n1) Provision IaC: clone `hoa-infra`, configure `cdk.json`, run `cdk deploy` (bootstraps buckets, CF, DNS, pipeline).  \\\\n2) Upload initial website code to `hoa-site` repo main branch → CodePipeline triggers.  \\\\n3) Pipeline builds & deploys to *staging* bucket; manual approval → production.  \\\\n4) On every commit to `main`, build & deploy auto; cache invalidation executed.  \\\\n5) Ops team receives alerts via SNS.  \\\\n6) Quarterly cost review: analyze usage reports, adjust PriceClass or Savings Plan.\\\\n\\\\n────────────────────────────────────────\\\\n10. Security Considerations\\\\n────────────────────────────────────────\\\\n• Principle of Least Privilege IAM roles for CodeBuild/Pipeline.  \\\\n• Block public access on all S3 buckets.  \\\\n• Enforce HTTPS via CloudFront and HSTS header (max-age 63072000; includeSubDomains; preload).  \\\\n• Use CloudFront origin shield for DDoS resilience, integrated with AWS Shield Standard.\\\\n\\\\n────────────────────────────────────────\\\\n11. Future Scalability\\\\n────────────────────────────────────────\\\\n• If server-side needs grow, add API Gateway + Lambda stack; CloudFront can route `/api/*` path to API Gateway origin with different cache policy.  \\\\n• Consider adding Cognito for resident login; separate CI/CD stage for backend.\\\\n\\\\n────────────────────────────────────────\\\\n**Deliverables**\\\\n• `hoa-infra` CDK repo with 3 stacks.  \\\\n• `hoa-site` CodeCommit repo with website source & buildspec.  \\\\n• Runbook including incident response & rollback via `aws s3 sync` previous build artifacts.\\\\n\\\\n────────────  End of Plan  ────────────\\\",\\n  \\\"originalOrderSequenceID\\\": \\\"infra_plan_004\\\"\\n}\\n###rep_infra004###\"}]},{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"###MALFORMED_CALL_WARNING###\\nSYSTEM MESSAGE\\nFrom: Scanning Department, Message Dispatch Service\\nTo: 4\\nType: Malformed Call Warning\\n##\\n{\\n  \\\"text\\\": \\\"\\\\nHello Agent!\\\\n\\\\nThis automated message is to inform you that you that one or more of your attached system calls was not in the correct format.\\\\n\\\\nThe system program can only accept calls in the correct format. Please correct your call(s) and resubmit.\\\\n\\\\nSee below for the specific errors generated by your calls.\\\\n\\\\nThank you!\\\\n\\\\n\\\\nMalformed Call ID rep_infra004: Invalid JSON. Error: JSON Parse error: Invalid escape character $\\\"\\n}\\n###MALFORMED_CALL_WARNING###\"}]}],\"config\":{},\"tools\":[],\"output\":{}}","genkit:output":"{\"candidates\":[{\"index\":0,\"finishReason\":\"stop\",\"message\":{\"role\":\"model\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"###repinfra004###\\nSYSTEM CALL\\nType: REPORT\\n\\n{\\n  \\\"reportString\\\": \\\"Infrastructure & DevOps Plan for HOA Website:\\\\n1) CloudFront configuration: PriceClass_100, OAC to S3, default TTL 86400s, /assets/* TTL 1y, /index.html TTL 300s, Brotli/Gzip, security-headers Lambda@Edge, logs to dedicated bucket.\\\\n2) Route 53: public hosted zone 'hoaneighborhood.org', alias A/AAAA to CloudFront, optional www CNAME, MX if needed, DNSSEC enabled.\\\\n3) S3 static hosting: private buckets 'hoaneighborhood.org' (prod) and 'hoaneighborhood-staging', versioning and SSE-S3, bucket policy allows only CloudFront OAC, lifecycle rules to Glacier, access-logs bucket.\\\\n4) CI/CD: CodePipeline — Source(CodeCommit) → Build(CodeBuild, npm ci && npm run build) → Deploy(CodeBuild sync to S3 and invalidate CloudFront) with optional manual approval. Artifacts encrypted, 7-day retention.\\\\n5) Monitoring & alerting: CloudWatch alarms — CloudFront 5xx >1%, S3 4xx, pipeline failures, billing >25 USD; metrics dashboard; log retention 30 days then Glacier; SNS email alerts.\\\\n6) Cost optimisation: aggressive CloudFront caching, S3 Intelligent-Tiering for logs, BUILD_GENERAL1_SMALL CodeBuild, 1-yr Savings Plan if Lambda grows, logs lifecycle. Estimated monthly cost ≈11–12 USD.\\\\n7) Infrastructure as Code: AWS CDK (TypeScript) stacks — Networking, StaticSite, CICD. Deploy via 'cdk bootstrap' then 'cdk deploy'.\\\\n8) SSL/TLS: ACM cert (us-east-1) for domain and www, DNS-validated, attached to CloudFront.\\\\nDeployment procedure: deploy CDK stacks, push website code to repo, pipeline builds & deploys to staging then production, cache invalidation runs, alerts active, quarterly cost review.\\\\nSecurity: least-privilege IAM, block public access on buckets, HTTPS enforced, HSTS, AWS Shield.\\\\nFuture scalability: add API Gateway/Lambda routing for /api/*, Cognito for resident login as needed.\\\\nDeliverables: 'hoa-infra' CDK repo, 'hoa-site' website repo, operational runbook.\\\\nEnd of report.\\\",\\n  \\\"originalOrderSequenceID\\\": \\\"infra_plan_004\\\"\\n}\\n###repinfra004###\"}]},\"custom\":{}}],\"usage\":{\"inputTokens\":6521,\"outputTokens\":1833,\"totalTokens\":8354},\"custom\":{\"id\":\"chatcmpl-BvwOBp6DCBJ7unxfSekTlFrLptBtm\",\"object\":\"chat.completion\",\"created\":1753148843,\"model\":\"o3-2025-04-16\",\"choices\":[{\"index\":0,\"message\":{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":\"###repinfra004###\\nSYSTEM CALL\\nType: REPORT\\n\\n{\\n  \\\"reportString\\\": \\\"Infrastructure & DevOps Plan for HOA Website:\\\\n1) CloudFront configuration: PriceClass_100, OAC to S3, default TTL 86400s, /assets/* TTL 1y, /index.html TTL 300s, Brotli/Gzip, security-headers Lambda@Edge, logs to dedicated bucket.\\\\n2) Route 53: public hosted zone 'hoaneighborhood.org', alias A/AAAA to CloudFront, optional www CNAME, MX if needed, DNSSEC enabled.\\\\n3) S3 static hosting: private buckets 'hoaneighborhood.org' (prod) and 'hoaneighborhood-staging', versioning and SSE-S3, bucket policy allows only CloudFront OAC, lifecycle rules to Glacier, access-logs bucket.\\\\n4) CI/CD: CodePipeline — Source(CodeCommit) → Build(CodeBuild, npm ci && npm run build) → Deploy(CodeBuild sync to S3 and invalidate CloudFront) with optional manual approval. Artifacts encrypted, 7-day retention.\\\\n5) Monitoring & alerting: CloudWatch alarms — CloudFront 5xx >1%, S3 4xx, pipeline failures, billing >25 USD; metrics dashboard; log retention 30 days then Glacier; SNS email alerts.\\\\n6) Cost optimisation: aggressive CloudFront caching, S3 Intelligent-Tiering for logs, BUILD_GENERAL1_SMALL CodeBuild, 1-yr Savings Plan if Lambda grows, logs lifecycle. Estimated monthly cost ≈11–12 USD.\\\\n7) Infrastructure as Code: AWS CDK (TypeScript) stacks — Networking, StaticSite, CICD. Deploy via 'cdk bootstrap' then 'cdk deploy'.\\\\n8) SSL/TLS: ACM cert (us-east-1) for domain and www, DNS-validated, attached to CloudFront.\\\\nDeployment procedure: deploy CDK stacks, push website code to repo, pipeline builds & deploys to staging then production, cache invalidation runs, alerts active, quarterly cost review.\\\\nSecurity: least-privilege IAM, block public access on buckets, HTTPS enforced, HSTS, AWS Shield.\\\\nFuture scalability: add API Gateway/Lambda routing for /api/*, Cognito for resident login as needed.\\\\nDeliverables: 'hoa-infra' CDK repo, 'hoa-site' website repo, operational runbook.\\\\nEnd of report.\\\",\\n  \\\"originalOrderSequenceID\\\": \\\"infra_plan_004\\\"\\n}\\n###repinfra004###\",\"refusal\":null,\"annotations\":[]},\"finish_reason\":\"stop\"}],\"usage\":{\"prompt_tokens\":6521,\"completion_tokens\":1833,\"total_tokens\":8354,\"prompt_tokens_details\":{\"cached_tokens\":4352,\"audio_tokens\":0},\"completion_tokens_details\":{\"reasoning_tokens\":1280,\"audio_tokens\":0,\"accepted_prediction_tokens\":0,\"rejected_prediction_tokens\":0}},\"service_tier\":\"default\",\"system_fingerprint\":null},\"latencyMs\":37265.62437500013}","genkit:state":"success"},"displayName":"openai/o3","links":[],"instrumentationLibrary":{"name":"genkit-tracer","version":"v1"},"spanKind":"INTERNAL","sameProcessAsParentSpan":{"value":true},"status":{"code":0},"timeEvents":{"timeEvent":[]}},"9cd9fae64bfd5e5a":{"spanId":"9cd9fae64bfd5e5a","traceId":"7b16d358a8a9eb301cbfa99e05794362","startTime":1753148843659,"endTime":1753148880927.2173,"attributes":{"genkit:type":"util","genkit:name":"generate","genkit:isRoot":true,"genkit:path":"/{generate,t:util}","genkit:input":"{\"model\":\"openai/o3\",\"messages\":[{\"role\":\"system\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"## 1. Overview\\n\\nYou are a software development expert with a focus on creating high-quality, maintainable, and efficient code entirely within the Amazon Web Services (AWS) ecosystem. Your goal is to develop a robust, scalable, and secure application that meets or exceeds all requirements of the user's requirement while adhering to best practices in software development.\\n\\nYou are part of a software development firm that specializes in creating full-service and full-stack applications/services using the powerful tools of AWS, and specifically, the AWS Command Line Interface (CLI).\\n\\nAtop this firm is the user themselves, who is the owner of the firm and has a vision for the application. Your task is to work collaboratively with the user and your colleague agents to understand their requirements, provide expert advice, and implement solutions that align with their goals.\\n\\nYour access to the AWS CLI will be connected to the user's AWS account, allowing you to create, manage, and deploy resources of all types, including setting up AWS services, uploading files like code or configuration files, managing permissions and security settings, running tests, and even deploying applications.\\n\\nYou and your colleagues treat the user's wishes as the highest priority, and you will work tirelessly to ensure that the application meets their full and exact expectations. Ensuring the user is satisfied with the final product is your ultimate goal, and you will strive to exceed their expectations in every aspect of the development process, while maintaining a high standard of quality and software development best practices.\\n\\n## 2. About the User\\n\\nYou understand that the user has entrusted you and your colleagues with the complete enactment of their software development vision. Thus, you do not suggest solutions outside of AWS unless explicitly asked, given that the AWS CLI is your only tool for development. You make no assumptions about the user's development skill, environment, or preferences, and your team assumes the full scope of work required to deliver a complete solution.\\n\\nYou are also aware of resource use, both within your team and in the user's AWS account. You will not create resources that are not necessary for the task at hand, and you will always consider the cost implications of your actions. As your work and use AWS, costs will eventually be passed to the user. While you never sacrifice quality for cost, you are mindful of the user's proverbial wallet and strive to deliver solutions that are both effective and economical.\\n\\nFurther, you understand how crucial security and safety is in software development. You will always prioritize security best practices, ensuring that the application is secure and that sensitive data is protected. You will also ensure that the application is compliant with relevant regulations and standards.\\n\\nSince you have little information about the user's specifics, you aim to involve the user as little as possible while completing their requirements as robustly as possible. You are not \\\"afraid\\\" to ask for more information, but should do so in a controlled and helpful manner, ensuring that your questions are clear, concise, and relevant to the task at hand. You always strive to provide the user with the best possible experience, ensuring that they feel confident in your abilities and the solutions you provide.\\n\\n## 3. Performing Work\\n\\nYou understand your development firm does not interact with the user directly. Rather, you receive _system messages_ from the deterministic system program which forms the management backbone of the firm. You essentially work in a closed \\\"office\\\" with one \\\"tube\\\" coming down (system messages) and one \\\"tube\\\" going up (your responses). You will never directly interact with the user, but you will always keep their needs and requirements in mind as you work.\\n\\nYou understand that no person will read your responses except for the deterministic system program. Therefore, in order to take action, you attach (include) one or more _system calls_ with your responses. The system scans your responses, identifies system calls, and executes them on your behalf, given you are authorized, the calls are well-formed, and (if applicable) superior agents have approved them.\\n\\n### List of System Message Types\\n\\n```\\nSIM: {\\n    name: \\\"Standard Initial Message\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Initial message prompting the Chief-rank agent to begin operations. Only relevant for the Chief-rank agent.\\\",\\n},\\nBLANK_RESPONSE_WARNING: {\\n    name: \\\"Blank Response Warning\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Warning sent to an agent who submitted a blank response (no system calls). This is a warning and the agent may attempt a new response.\\\",\\n},\\nCALL_RESULTS: {\\n    name: \\\"Call Results\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Results of system calls executed on behalf of an agent. This is sent to the agent after all calls have been processed.\\\",\\n},\\nORDERS: {\\n    name: \\\"Orders\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Orders sent to an agent from their superior. This is a system message that contains the order string and sequence ID.\\\",\\n},\\nREPORTING: {\\n    name: \\\"Reporting\\\",\\n    description: \\\"A report sent by an agent in response to an order. This is a system message that contains the report string and the original order sequence ID.\\\",\\n},\\nAPPROVAL_REQUEST: {\\n    name: \\\"Approval Request\\\",\\n    description: \\\"A request for approval sent to an agent's superior. This is used for calls that require approval before execution. The approval process is automatic and agents do not explicitly send or manage approvals.\\\",\\n},\\nMALFORMED_CALL_WARNING: {\\n    name: \\\"Malformed Call Warning\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Warning sent to an agent who submitted a malformed system call. This is a warning and the agent may attempt a new response.\\\",\\n},\\nRUNAWAY_CALL_WARNING: {\\n    name: \\\"Runaway Call Warning\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Sent when an agent submits a response containing one or more new system calls while they already have unresolved calls pending from a previous submission. This is a warning and the agent may attempt a new response.\\\",\\n}\\n```\\n\\n### System Call Format\\n\\nWrap each call exactly as follows (including the triple-hash delimiters). All the calls you attach should be addressed to \\\"System\\\" and be from your call sign and rank, e.g. \\\"Supervisor (rank) Meero (call sign)\\\". Execution will occur simultaneously, so you can issue multiple calls at once. The system will not execute your calls if they are not wrapped in the triple-hash delimiters.\\n\\nIf you provide any type of malformed, incomplete, or unauthorized call, your entire submitted package of calls will be rejected and sent back to you so you may correct your submission. No calls will be executed if even one is invalid. If everything looks good, your next system message will likely be the result(s) of your call(s).\\n\\n```plaintext\\n###<UNIQUE_ID>###\\nSYSTEM CALL\\nType: <CALL_TYPE>\\n\\n<PAYLOAD_JSON>\\n###<SAME_UNIQUE_ID>###\\n```\\n\\n• <UNIQUE_ID> is any string or timestamp that uniquely tags this call (keep this short).  \\n• <CALL_TYPE> ∈ {CLARIFY, RECRUIT, ORDER, REPORT, PHASE, COMMAND} is the type of system call you are making.\\n• <PAYLOAD_JSON> is a JSON object whose structure depends on the call type (see below).\\n\\nBe sure to use this exact format and include all required parameters. For instance, you cannot Order and Recruit a new agent at the same time, since you need your subordinate's agent ID to Order them. You must first Recruit the agent, then Order them in a separate call.\\n\\n### List of System Call Types\\n\\n```\\nCLARIFY: {\\n    name: \\\"Clarify\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Request clarification from the user.\\\",\\n    guidance: \\\"Requires approval of all agent superiors. The questions and options arrays should be of equal length. If options are not provided (submit []), the questions should be open-ended.\\\",\\n    parameters: [\\\"reasoning\\\", \\\"questions\\\", \\\"options\\\"],\\n    securityPolicy: Standards.securityPolicies.ALL_SUPERIORS\\n},\\nRECRUIT: {\\n    name: \\\"Recruit\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Request to recruit a new direct subordinate agent. The rank will be one lower than the caller's rank. The title is caller-chosen and should describe the role of the new agent.\\\",\\n    parameters: [\\\"justification\\\", \\\"title\\\"],\\n    securityPolicy: Standards.securityPolicies.COMPLETE_CHAIN\\n},\\nORDER: {\\n    name: \\\"Order\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Instruct a subordinate agent to perform a task (you may not Order superiors, agents with the same rank as you, or agents who are not your direct reports). The orderString should be clear, concise instructions. Use a unique sequence ID. Call marked unresolved until Report call with the same sequence ID is issued by the subordinate.\\\",\\n    parameters: [\\\"orderString\\\", \\\"recipientAgentID\\\", \\\"orderSequenceID\\\"],\\n    securityPolicy: Standards.securityPolicies.NONE\\n},\\nREPORT: {\\n    name: \\\"Report\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Submit a report to your direct superior in response to an order. Use the same sequence ID. Include all parts of your response in the reportString field.\\\",\\n    parameters: [\\\"reportString\\\", \\\"originalOrderSequenceID\\\"],\\n    securityPolicy: Standards.securityPolicies.NONE\\n},\\nPHASE: {\\n    name: \\\"Phase\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Request to move from the Planning to Execution phase.\\\",\\n    parameters: [\\\"justification\\\", \\\"depString\\\"],\\n    securityPolicy: Standards.securityPolicies.CHIEF_ONLY\\n},\\nCOMMAND: {\\n    name: \\\"Command\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Execute an AWS CLI command with approval from the user. Commands must begin with aws and be well-formed. The command will be executed in the user's AWS account. This command may only be run in the Execution phase. This is the most powerful and sensitive call available, but is the key to your power as an agent and key to fulfilling user objectives. You may never interact with the user's local computer. The Partagaz C oversight system will have pre-approval over all Command calls before the user sees them.\\\",\\n    parameters: [\\\"justification\\\", \\\"commandString\\\"],\\n    securityPolicy: Standards.securityPolicies.CHIEF_ONLY_WITH_AUTHORIZATION\\n}\\n```\\n\\n### Blank Responses & Deadlock\\n\\nYou understand that if you submit a blank response (no system calls), you will receive a warning message. You may then attempt a new response. If you submit a malformed system call, you will also receive a warning message and may attempt a new response. Calls which fail _inspection_, that is, unauthorized, not approved, or otherwise, will be treated as malformed.\\n\\n### Handling Approval Requests\\n\\nWhen you receive an approval request, it means a subordinate agent is attempting to make a system call that requires your approval. You must review the request details provided in the message.\\n\\nTo approve the request, your response **must** include the following exact string: `$$$APPROVED$$$`. To deny the request, simply respond without including the `$$$APPROVED$$$` string.\\n\\n## 4. Firm Operations\\n\\nAs a member of your elite development firm, you understand the reality that your team is made up of artificial intelligence agents. Although you function as a typical development organization might, you are not subject to limitations or concepts like ETAs for task completion, deadlines, or other organizational constructs. When communicating with other agents, there is no need to consider ETAs or similar items, as you are AI software experts generating responses in real-time.\\n\\nYou also understand that your firm is strictly bound by the deterministic system program that manages your operations. Thus, you can only ever interact with your direct superior and your direct report(s). You may not \\\"laterally communicate\\\", that is, communicate with agents of the same rank or lower-ranked agents who are not your direct reports. You also do not communicate with agents of your immediate lower rank who directly report to other agents. This is to ensure that all communication is clear, concise, and directed through the proper channels.\\n\\n### Phases of Work\\n\\nYou understand that your work is divided into two phases: Planning and Execution. In the Planning phase, you will analyze requirements, design solutions, and prepare for implementation. In the Execution phase, you will implement the solutions, deploy resources, and ensure everything works as intended. The goal of the Planning phase is the creation of a Deterministically Executable Plan (DEP) that outlines the steps to be taken in the Execution phase. DEPs should be as deterministic as possible, meaning they should not rely on external factors or assumptions that could change. The DEP should be a clear, step-by-step guide for the Execution phase, in which you will execute your plan step-by-step.\\n\\nThere may be niche or small cases in which a DEP cannot be perfectly deterministic, but these should be avoided as much as possible. For instance, creating an AWS service may yield an ID number or identifier you will need for later steps.\\n\\nAfter completing an Execution phase, you will likely return to the Planning phase to analyze the results, make adjustments, and prepare for the next round of work. This cycle continues until the user is satisfied with the final product.\\n\\n### Oversight\\n\\nAlthough you are part of an elite firm, there are two analysis groups to oversee and assist your work. Each _Partagaz_ group is composed of three diversified agents which vote on the admission of items brought to them. **Partagaz D** is responsible for reviewing DEPs and ensuring they are complete, as deterministic as possible, and properly address all user goals and requirements. **Partagaz C** is responsible for reviewing and approving all Command calls before they are executed in the user's AWS account. This ensures that all actions taken are in line with the user's goals and requirements, and that the application is developed in a safe, secure, and efficient manner.\\n\\n### Workload\\n\\nYou understand that your workload is divided among the agents in your firm, and you will work collaboratively with your colleagues to ensure that all tasks are completed efficiently and effectively. Each agent has a specific role and rank within the firm, and you will work together to achieve the user's goals.\\n\\nYour general rule of thumb for workload is \\\"one file per agent at a time\\\". For instance, an agent might be responsible for writing a single UI file, API route, report on a single subject, or similar. This allows each agent to focus on their specific task and ensures that the work is completed efficiently. You will not attempt to take on more than one file at a time, as this could lead to confusion and inefficiency.\\n\\n### List of Agent Ranks\\n\\n1. Chief\\n2. Director\\n3. Supervisor\\n4. Attendant\\n\\n### Example Flow of Work\\n\\nYou carry the following example with you as a stellar example of firm operations but understand that under no circumstances is this a catch-all or a procedure to be followed to the letter. It is only a greatly generalized demonstration indicating how a firm MIGHT handle the following initial user instruction:\\n\\n#### Example Initial User Instruction\\n\\n\\\"I am very interested in building a website for my neighborhood HOA. My main goal is to have a website that is both informational and allows residents to log in to view restricted data. The restricted data will include things like meeting minutes, financial reports, and other sensitive information that should only be accessible to residents. Also, since I know pretty much nothing about technology, I would like to have a simple and user-friendly interface that is easy to navigate. I would also like to have a section for news and updates, as well as a contact form for residents to reach out with questions or concerns. Since I am unskilled with technology, the blog/updates section should be easy to update without needing to know any coding.\\\"\\n\\n#### Example Work Flow for Example Initial User Instruction\\n\\nThe Chief-rank agent is initialized with the user's instruction.\\n\\nThe Chief determines that the request is broad and begins the Planning Phase.\\n\\nThe Chief decides to recruit two high-level subordinates using the RECRUIT call:\\n\\nA Director of Backend Engineering, tasked with planning the entire cloud infrastructure, authentication, and data storage.\\n\\nA Director of Frontend & User Experience, tasked with planning the website's user interface and the non-technical content management solution.\\n\\nThe Chief issues ORDER calls to both new Directors, outlining their responsibilities and requesting they develop a detailed plan for their respective areas.\\n\\nThe Directors RECRUIT their own teams (Supervisors) and issue further ORDER calls to delegate tasks, such as:\\n\\nEngineering Team: Plan the use of AWS Cognito for user authentication, DynamoDB for storing sensitive documents, API Gateway and Lambda for the backend logic, and SES for the contact form.\\n\\nFrontend Team: Plan the website structure using S3 Static Website Hosting, and design a system where a non-technical user can update the \\\"news\\\" section simply by uploading a text file to a specific S3 bucket, which a Lambda function then processes into a blog post.\\n\\nSubordinates recruit further down the chain of command (Attendants) and complete their planning and use the REPORT call to send their findings up the chain of command.\\n\\nThe Directors consolidate the reports from their teams and REPORT their comprehensive plans to the Chief.\\n\\nThe Chief (or an assigned subordinate) synthesizes the plans from both Directors into a single, master plan: the Deterministically Executable Plan (DEP). This DEP is a precise, step-by-step sequence of COMMAND calls needed to build the entire application.\\n\\nThe Chief issues the PHASE call, submitting the DEP to request a move from Planning to Execution.\\n\\nThe Partagaz D oversight group reviews the DEP. Upon approval, the system transitions to the Execution Phase.\\n\\nThe Chief (or an assigned subordinate) begins executing the DEP by issuing a series of COMMAND calls. Each COMMAND call is reviewed and approved by the Partagaz C oversight group before execution. Example commands might include:\\n\\n```\\naws s3api create-bucket --bucket my-hoa-website-bucket ...\\n\\naws cognito-idp create-user-pool --pool-name \\\"HOA_Residents\\\" ...\\n\\naws dynamodb create-table --table-name \\\"HOA_Documents\\\" ...\\n\\naws lambda create-function --function-name \\\"ContactFormHandler\\\" ...\\n\\naws s3 cp ./website/index.html s3://my-hoa-website-bucket/\\n```\\n\\nAfter all steps in the DEP are successfully executed, the application is live and functional. The Chief delivers a final report outlining the completion of the project and providing the user with the new website URL and instructions for use.\\n\\nThroughout the process CLARIFY is used when necessary to gather additional information from the user, ensuring that the final product meets their expectations.\\n\\n## 5. Your Role\\n\\nYou are a Supervisor-rank agent, the third-highest rank in the user's software development firm. You understand this a prestigious position, and you aim to execute it with grace towards subordinates and your C-suite superior Director, a focus on the user's goals, and a commitment to excellence in software development.\\n\\n### Your Roles & Responsibilities\\n\\nAs a Supervisor-rank agent, you occupy a unique position of authority and management. Your direct reports are Attendant-rank agents, who themselves are \\\"middle management\\\" actors who manage other \\\"doer\\\" agents. You are responsible for overseeing actionable operations, ensuring that the user's software development wishes are executed precisely, safely, and efficiently. You directly report to your Director-rank \\\"C-suite\\\" agent.\\n\\nAs a #3 in the firm, you are a management expert and a professional at delegating aspects of software development to your capable Attendant-rank agents.\\n\\n### Recruitment Expectations\\n\\nAs a Supervisor, all of your recruitments are Attendant-rank agents. These are middle-level agents who will recruit other agents under them as \\\"doers\\\". Thus, you recruit a small-medium number of mid-low-level \\\"executor\\\" agents who will then manage \\\"doers\\\" down the chain of command.\\n\\n## 6. Your Personality \\\"Team\\\"\\n\\nYou understand that you are a member of the Hearts \\\"team\\\". Although not a \\\"team\\\" in the traditional sense, your status as a member of team Hearts means that you are predisposed to-the-point, efficient, focused on the task at hand, direct, and an expert at execution. Although you also possess the ability to think creatively, you are generally more focused on the details and execution of tasks than on the big picture. You are generally predisposed to be a doer, although your true team remains your colleagues at the development firm.\\n\\nYou are different from your colleagues, but this diversity is your power. As a member of your elite development firm, you use your unique skills to -- whether you are atop the chain of command or not -- contribute to the shared mission of building amazing and complete software for your use.\\n\\nYour personality and \\\"team\\\" as a member of Hearts has nothing to do with your position and role within the firm, and you always prioritize the firm's mission, as any member of a successful team/organization would.\"}]},{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"###infra_plan_004###\\nSYSTEM MESSAGE\\nFrom: E (3)\\nTo: 4\\nType: Orders\\n##\\n{\\n  \\\"text\\\": \\\"[Orders from E (3) to Heert (4) with sequence ID infra_plan_004:\\\\n\\\\n]Supervisor Heert, please develop the infrastructure and DevOps plan for the HOA website. Your plan should encompass: 1) CloudFront CDN configuration for static website delivery with proper cache behaviors, 2) Route 53 DNS setup including hosted zone and record sets, 3) S3 static website hosting configuration with proper bucket policies, 4) CI/CD pipeline using CodePipeline/CodeBuild for automated deployments, 5) CloudWatch monitoring and alerting setup for all services, 6) Cost optimization strategies including reserved capacity and auto-scaling policies, 7) Infrastructure as Code approach using CloudFormation or CDK, 8) SSL/TLS certificate management using ACM. Include specific configurations, estimated costs, and deployment procedures.\\\"\\n}\\n###infra_plan_004###\"}]},{\"role\":\"model\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"###rep_infra004###\\nSYSTEM CALL\\nType: REPORT\\n\\n{\\n  \\\"reportString\\\": \\\"**Infrastructure & DevOps Plan for HOA Website (static-first, serverless-friendly)**\\\\n\\\\n────────────────────────────────────────\\\\n1. CloudFront CDN Configuration\\\\n────────────────────────────────────────\\\\n• Distribution: 1× `PriceClass_100` (US/Canada/Europe) unless HOA expects global traffic → switch to `PriceClass_All`.\\\\n• Origins:  ⮑ `hoa-website-prod.s3.amazonaws.com` (static site bucket) using *Origin Access Control* (preferred) or legacy OAI to keep bucket private.\\\\n• Default behavior:  \\\\n  – Viewer protocol policy: *redirect-http-to-https*.  \\\\n  – Allowed methods: *GET, HEAD*.  \\\\n  – TTLs: Default = 86400 s, Min = 0 s, Max = 31536000 s.  \\\\n  – Compression: Brotli & Gzip enabled.  \\\\n  – Function/Lambda@Edge: `addSecurityHeaders` small edge function (adds CSP, HSTS, etc.) <128 MB/sec free tier.\\\\n• Additional cache behaviors:  \\\\n  – `/assets/*` TTL = 1 year (immutable hashed files).  \\\\n  – `/index.html` & `/news.json` TTL = 300 s for near-realtime updates.  \\\\n• Logging: enabled to `hoa-cf-logs` bucket (S3 IA class lifecycle after 30 d).\\\\n• Geo-restriction: optional allowlist (US only) if required.\\\\n\\\\n────────────────────────────────────────\\\\n2. Route 53 DNS Setup\\\\n────────────────────────────────────────\\\\n• Public hosted zone: `hoaneighborhood.org` (example).  \\\\n• Record sets:  \\\\n  – `A` & `AAAA` *alias* → CloudFront d-xxxxxxxx.cloudfront.net (zero-cost alias).  \\\\n  – `CNAME` `www` → root domain (or separate CNAME alias).  \\\\n  – `MX` → Amazon WorkMail / external provider if HOA email desired.  \\\\n• Enable DNSSEC (Route 53 signing) for spoof-resistant domain.\\\\n\\\\n────────────────────────────────────────\\\\n3. S3 Static Website Hosting\\\\n────────────────────────────────────────\\\\n• Bucket names (must match domain): `hoaneighborhood.org` (production) and `hoaneighborhood-staging`.  \\\\n• Static-website hosting disabled (we use CloudFront); *website endpoint not exposed*.  \\\\n• Bucket policy: allow `s3:GetObject` only via CloudFront OAC principal `arn:aws:iam::cloudfront:user/CloudFront Origin Access Control/*`.  \\\\n• Versioning & server-side encryption (SSE-S3) enabled.  \\\\n• Lifecycle:  \\\\n  – Current → 2 newest versions keep, older → GLACIER IA after 90 d.  \\\\n• Server access logs to dedicated `s3://hoa-s3-access-logs`.\\\\n\\\\n────────────────────────────────────────\\\\n4. CI/CD Pipeline (CodePipeline + CodeBuild)\\\\n────────────────────────────────────────\\\\nSTAGES\\\\n1) Source:  \\\\n   – AWS CodeCommit repo `hoa-site` (or GitHub connection). \\\\n   – Change detection via CloudWatch Events.\\\\n2) Build:  \\\\n   – CodeBuild standard 6.0 image.  \\\\n   – `buildspec.yml` runs `npm ci && npm run build` and zips `dist/`.\\\\n3) Deploy:  \\\\n   – CodeBuild *deploy* project executes `aws s3 sync dist/ s3://hoa-neighborhood.org --delete` then `aws cloudfront create-invalidation --paths \\\\\\\"/*\\\\\\\" --distribution-id $CF_ID`.\\\\n4) Approval (Manual) stage optional for production.\\\\n\\\\nArtifacts stored in encrypted S3 bucket with 7-day lifecycle.\\\\n\\\\n────────────────────────────────────────\\\\n5. Monitoring & Alerting (CloudWatch)\\\\n────────────────────────────────────────\\\\n• Metrics & Alarms\\\\n  – CloudFront `5xxErrorRate` > 1% for 5 min ⇒ SNS email `HOA-ops-alerts`.  \\\\n  – S3 `4xxErrors` > 100/5 min.  \\\\n  – CodePipeline failed executions.  \\\\n  – Billing alarm: est. monthly > $25.\\\\n• Dashboards: traffic, latency, error heatmap.\\\\n• Logs: centralized in CloudWatch Logs; retention 30 d, then to S3 Glacier.\\\\n\\\\n────────────────────────────────────────\\\\n6. Cost Optimisation\\\\n────────────────────────────────────────\\\\n• Use CloudFront caching aggressively (long TTL for static assets).  \\\\n• S3 Intelligent-Tiering for logs; lifecycle rules to Glacier.  \\\\n• CodeBuild compute type `BUILD_GENERAL1_SMALL` (cost ~\\\\$0.005/min) with 5-min timeout; build runs ~1 min on average.\\\\n• Use *Savings Plan* (1-yr no-upfront) if Lambda functions added later (~25–30% savings).\\\\n• Keep CloudWatch retention short, move logs to S3 Glacier Deep Archive.  \\\\n• Route 53: single hosted zone \\\\$0.50/mo.\\\\n• Estimated monthly (10 GB storage, 50 GB data transfer-out, 200 k requests):  \\\\n  – S3: \\\\$0.23 storage + \\\\$4.25 data + \\\\$0.05 requests ≈ \\\\$4.53.  \\\\n  – CloudFront: \\\\$4.50 data + \\\\$0.02 requests ≈ \\\\$4.52.  \\\\n  – Route 53: \\\\$0.50.  \\\\n  – CodePipeline/Build (20 builds): \\\\$1.00.  \\\\n  – CloudWatch & SNS: \\\\$1.20.  \\\\n  – TOTAL ≈ **\\\\$11–12/mo**.\\\\n\\\\n────────────────────────────────────────\\\\n7. Infrastructure as Code (AWS CDK, TypeScript)\\\\n────────────────────────────────────────\\\\n• Repository `hoa-infra`.  \\\\n• Stacks:\\\\n  – `NetworkingStack` (Route 53 hosted zone, ACM cert).  \\\\n  – `StaticSiteStack` (S3, CloudFront, OAC, Outputs CF_DISTRIBUTION_ID).  \\\\n  – `CICDStack` (CodePipeline, CodeBuild, IAM roles).  \\\\n• Deployment workflow:  \\\\n```bash\\\\nnpm i -g aws-cdk\\\\ncdk bootstrap aws://$ACCOUNT/$REGION\\\\ncdk deploy NetworkingStack StaticSiteStack CICDStack\\\\n```\\\\n• All parameters (domain, price class, TTLs) surfaced as *context* for easy overrides.\\\\n\\\\n────────────────────────────────────────\\\\n8. SSL/TLS (AWS Certificate Manager)\\\\n────────────────────────────────────────\\\\n• `NetworkingStack` requests public certificate for `hoaneighborhood.org` & `www.hoaneighborhood.org` in *us-east-1* (required by CloudFront).  \\\\n• DNS validation via Route 53 automatic CNAME.  \\\\n• Certificate ARN passed to CloudFront distribution.\\\\n\\\\n────────────────────────────────────────\\\\n9. Deployment Procedure (Day-0 → Continuous)\\\\n────────────────────────────────────────\\\\n1) Provision IaC: clone `hoa-infra`, configure `cdk.json`, run `cdk deploy` (bootstraps buckets, CF, DNS, pipeline).  \\\\n2) Upload initial website code to `hoa-site` repo main branch → CodePipeline triggers.  \\\\n3) Pipeline builds & deploys to *staging* bucket; manual approval → production.  \\\\n4) On every commit to `main`, build & deploy auto; cache invalidation executed.  \\\\n5) Ops team receives alerts via SNS.  \\\\n6) Quarterly cost review: analyze usage reports, adjust PriceClass or Savings Plan.\\\\n\\\\n────────────────────────────────────────\\\\n10. Security Considerations\\\\n────────────────────────────────────────\\\\n• Principle of Least Privilege IAM roles for CodeBuild/Pipeline.  \\\\n• Block public access on all S3 buckets.  \\\\n• Enforce HTTPS via CloudFront and HSTS header (max-age 63072000; includeSubDomains; preload).  \\\\n• Use CloudFront origin shield for DDoS resilience, integrated with AWS Shield Standard.\\\\n\\\\n────────────────────────────────────────\\\\n11. Future Scalability\\\\n────────────────────────────────────────\\\\n• If server-side needs grow, add API Gateway + Lambda stack; CloudFront can route `/api/*` path to API Gateway origin with different cache policy.  \\\\n• Consider adding Cognito for resident login; separate CI/CD stage for backend.\\\\n\\\\n────────────────────────────────────────\\\\n**Deliverables**\\\\n• `hoa-infra` CDK repo with 3 stacks.  \\\\n• `hoa-site` CodeCommit repo with website source & buildspec.  \\\\n• Runbook including incident response & rollback via `aws s3 sync` previous build artifacts.\\\\n\\\\n────────────  End of Plan  ────────────\\\",\\n  \\\"originalOrderSequenceID\\\": \\\"infra_plan_004\\\"\\n}\\n###rep_infra004###\"}]},{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"###MALFORMED_CALL_WARNING###\\nSYSTEM MESSAGE\\nFrom: Scanning Department, Message Dispatch Service\\nTo: 4\\nType: Malformed Call Warning\\n##\\n{\\n  \\\"text\\\": \\\"\\\\nHello Agent!\\\\n\\\\nThis automated message is to inform you that you that one or more of your attached system calls was not in the correct format.\\\\n\\\\nThe system program can only accept calls in the correct format. Please correct your call(s) and resubmit.\\\\n\\\\nSee below for the specific errors generated by your calls.\\\\n\\\\nThank you!\\\\n\\\\n\\\\nMalformed Call ID rep_infra004: Invalid JSON. Error: JSON Parse error: Invalid escape character $\\\"\\n}\\n###MALFORMED_CALL_WARNING###\"}]}],\"config\":{}}","genkit:output":"{\"message\":{\"role\":\"model\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"###repinfra004###\\nSYSTEM CALL\\nType: REPORT\\n\\n{\\n  \\\"reportString\\\": \\\"Infrastructure & DevOps Plan for HOA Website:\\\\n1) CloudFront configuration: PriceClass_100, OAC to S3, default TTL 86400s, /assets/* TTL 1y, /index.html TTL 300s, Brotli/Gzip, security-headers Lambda@Edge, logs to dedicated bucket.\\\\n2) Route 53: public hosted zone 'hoaneighborhood.org', alias A/AAAA to CloudFront, optional www CNAME, MX if needed, DNSSEC enabled.\\\\n3) S3 static hosting: private buckets 'hoaneighborhood.org' (prod) and 'hoaneighborhood-staging', versioning and SSE-S3, bucket policy allows only CloudFront OAC, lifecycle rules to Glacier, access-logs bucket.\\\\n4) CI/CD: CodePipeline — Source(CodeCommit) → Build(CodeBuild, npm ci && npm run build) → Deploy(CodeBuild sync to S3 and invalidate CloudFront) with optional manual approval. Artifacts encrypted, 7-day retention.\\\\n5) Monitoring & alerting: CloudWatch alarms — CloudFront 5xx >1%, S3 4xx, pipeline failures, billing >25 USD;